course: dictionary
lesson: 1946
title: Many meanings of common
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word common has more than one meaning, so you need its context to know which one is meant. «The common pheasant in British fields was brought from Asia long ago for hunting.» Here common means found in many places, so it is not rare. Ask yourself what the sentence is about, then choose the meaning that fits.
explain: «All these rules have one thing in common: they judge the person, not the act.» In common means shared, the same for each of them. Test it by swapping in shared: «one thing shared» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Common can also be a noun. A common is an open grassy area in a town that everyone can use. Maths uses common too: a common factor is a number that goes exactly into two or more numbers. When a word has many meanings, read the words around it and try each meaning in the sentence.
fact: Common means happening often or found in many places, as in a common bird.
fact: Common means shared by two or more, as in «one thing in common».
fact: Common means ordinary, not special, as in the common people.
fact: A common is an open grassy area in a town that everyone can use.
fact: In maths, a common factor goes exactly into two or more numbers: 3 is a common factor of 6 and 9.
exercise: «The common pheasant in British fields was brought from Asia long ago for hunting.» What does common mean here? | Very loud and noisy | A grassy space in a town | Shared by two or more people | *Found in many places, not rare
exercise: «All these rules have one thing in common: they judge the person, not the act.» What does in common mean? | Very loud | *Shared, the same for each one | Found in a field | Rare and special
exercise: What is a common factor of 6 and 9? | *3 | 2 | 4 | 5
test: Mia says, «Let's meet on the common by the school.» What is a common? | A type of bird | A kind of sum | A cake shop | *An open grassy area in a town that everyone can use
test: «Common sense tells you not to touch a hot pan.» What does common sense mean? | Loud shouting | A sense you only learn at school | *Good judgement that most people have | A rare, special talent
exam: Which sentence uses common to mean happening often? | *«Colds are common in winter.» | «The children played on the common.» | «The common people had no say in the matter.» | «She has one thing in common with her sister.»
